<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/tree-on-house.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/tree-on-house-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/tree-on-house.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="675" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><a href="/appraisal-work/">Appraisal work</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><span aria-current="page">Fallen Tree Damage Appraisal</span></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal work</p><h1>Fallen Tree Damage Claim Appraisal in Maine</h1><p class="lede">Maine is the most heavily forested state in the country, and a fallen tree damage claim in Maine is common after ice storms, nor’easters and summer squalls. When a white pine or red oak lands on a roof, the damage usually goes deeper than the shingles.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-coral" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg><span>Call (844) 537-1427</span></a><a class="btn btn-line light" href="/reach-us/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="spec"><div><dt>Common in</dt><dd>Storms year-round</dd></div><div><dt>Typical issue</dt><dd>Framing damage missed</dd></div><div><dt>Our role</dt><dd>Your named appraiser</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><h2>Why tree claims are so common here</h2>
<p>Houses across Maine sit close to tall white pine, spruce, hemlock, red oak and maple. Ice loading, heavy wet snow, saturated ground and high winds all bring trees down. The Ice Storm of January 1998 left much of central and southern Maine without power, some for weeks, with damage from fallen trees and limbs everywhere. The October 2017 windstorm and the December 2023 storm brought down thousands more. A fallen tree damage claim in Maine is often part of a wider storm claim, so see our <a href="/appraisal-work/windstorm-microburst-appraisal/">windstorm appraisal</a> and <a href="/appraisal-work/ice-dam-snow-load-appraisal/">ice dam and snow load appraisal</a> pages too.</p>

<h2>What carrier tree estimates commonly miss</h2>
<ul>
<li>Cracked rafters, ridge boards, ceiling joists and wall studs hidden under undamaged-looking finishes.</li>
<li>Roof sheathing replacement beyond the obvious hole, and full slope replacement where shingles cannot be matched.</li>
<li>Interior damage from rain entering before the roof was tarped.</li>
<li>Crane or specialized equipment to remove a large tree from a structure safely.</li>
<li>Gutters, siding, decks, windows and fencing damaged by the impact.</li>
<li>Engineering review for structural repairs.</li>
<li>Additional living expenses if the house is unsafe.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Tree removal and policy limits</h2>
<p>Most homeowners policies pay to remove a tree that damaged a covered structure, often with a limit per tree and per loss. Trees that fall without hitting a structure may not be covered unless they block a driveway or ramp, depending on wording. The cost of the structural repair is separate from the removal limit. Read your policy for the specifics, and be cautious about any argument that the tree was rotten or poorly maintained; if a carrier denies on that basis, it is a coverage question for a Maine attorney.</p>

<div class="note"><b class="h">Safety first, then evidence</b><p>Do not climb on a damaged roof or try to cut a tree resting on a structure. Photograph the tree in place from several angles before removal, including the stump and root ball, which help show the tree was healthy. Keep the tree service invoice, including any crane charges.</p></div>

<h2>Documentation that helps</h2>
<p>Photograph the impact from outside and inside, including attic framing. Ask the tree service to photograph any structural damage revealed during removal. Get a contractor or engineer to inspect framing. Keep receipts for tarps, emergency labor and hotel stays. For camps and second homes reached by long dirt roads or by water, record access issues that add cost.</p>

<h2>How appraisal applies to tree claims</h2>
<p>When the carrier accepts the claim but scopes it as a small roof patch, appraisal sets the amount. We inspect the framing, assess whether the slope can be matched, and price structural repairs as they really are, including winter work and access. We also make sure the estimate separates tree removal, which may have its own limit, from the building repair.</p>

<h3>Illustrative example</h3>
<p>Illustrative example: a red oak limb falls on a Presque Isle home during an ice storm. The carrier pays $8,300 for shingles, sheathing and a ceiling patch. The owner’s contractor finds three cracked rafters and a damaged wall plate, and the estimate totals $26,900 including engineering, framing and interior work. That scope gap is what appraisal addresses. Figures are invented for illustration.</p>

<h2>Serving the whole state</h2>
<p>Tree claims often arrive in clusters after a big storm, when tree services and roofers are booked for weeks and prices rise. A realistic estimate reflects those conditions rather than a quiet-season rate. We handle tree claims from York County to Aroostook. See our <a href="/towns-we-cover/presque-isle-maine/">Presque Isle page</a> for northern Maine specifics.</p>
<h2>Questions about fallen tree damage appraisal</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Does my policy cover a tree that fell on my house?</summary><p>Most homeowners policies cover damage to the structure and include a limited amount for tree removal. Read your policy for the specific limits.</p></details><details><summary>What if the tree was on my neighbor’s property?</summary><p>Your policy usually covers your house regardless of where the tree came from. Questions about a neighbor’s liability are legal ones for an attorney.</p></details><details><summary>Can a fallen tree damage claim in Maine include crane costs?</summary><p>Often yes, when a crane is needed to safely remove a tree from a structure. Keep the invoice and photos showing why it was needed.</p></details><details><summary>The carrier says the tree was rotten. What now?</summary><p>That is a coverage argument, not an amount dispute. Talk to a Maine attorney, and keep photos of the stump and trunk.</p></details></div>
